Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a batch-friendly histogram with fast max-tracking, enabling threshold queries without rescanning. It mirrors real-world monitoring workloads where high-volume time-bucketed signals must be maintained with tight per-update costs.

Key Concepts
- Histogram bucketing and width trade-offs
- Segment tree for max-tracking over buckets
- Batch amortization and lazy refresh strategies
- Space efficiency: O(S) vs. O(N)

Code Example
```javascript
class TimeHistogram {
  constructor(T, W) {
    this.S = Math.ceil(T / W); this.W = W; this.counts = new Array(this.S).fill(0);
    // build a simple segment tree for max over counts
    this.seg = new SegmentTree(this.counts);
  }
  apply(t, delta) { const b = Math.floor(t / this.W); this.counts[b] += delta; this.seg.update(b, this.counts[b]); }
  batchUpdate(updates) { for (const {t, d} of updates) this.apply(t, d); }
  hasHeavyBucket(M) { return this.seg.max() >= M; }
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How does changing W affect S and latency? 
- How would you adapt for dynamic T or non-uniform bucket widths?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your ability to design efficient dynamic ranking systems with batched updates, requiring you to balance update locality against query efficiency in real-world scenarios.

## Key Concepts
- Ordered maps for score-based organization
- Lazy maximum score tracking to avoid unnecessary recomputation
- Efficient top-K extraction through bucket traversal
- Amortized time complexity analysis
- Memory usage optimization techniques

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode implementation
class TopKKeeper {
  constructor(users) {
    this.scoreToIds = new TreeMap(); // score -> TreeSet of user IDs
    this.userToScore = new HashMap(); // user ID -> current score
    this.maxScore = null;

    // Initialize in O(n)
    for (const [id, score] of users) {
      this.addUserToBucket(id, score);
    }
  }

  updateBatch(updates) {
    // Process B updates in O(B log n)
    for (const [id, delta] of updates) {
      const oldScore = this.userToScore.get(id);
      const newScore = oldScore + delta;

      this.removeUserFromBucket(id, oldScore);
      this.addUserToBucket(id, newScore);
    }
  }

  getTopK(k) {
    // Extract top-K in O(K log n)
    const result = [];
    let currentScore = this.maxScore;
    let remaining = k;

    while (remaining > 0 && currentScore !== null) {
      const ids = this.scoreToIds.get(currentScore);
      const takeCount = Math.min(remaining, ids.size());

      // Extract first 'takeCount' IDs (already sorted)
      result.push(...Array.from(ids).slice(0, takeCount));
      remaining -= takeCount;
      currentScore = this.scoreToIds.lowerKey(currentScore);
    }

    return result.slice(0, k);
  }
}
```

## Optimizations
1. **Lazy Maximum Updates**: Only recompute the maximum score when the current maximum bucket becomes empty, avoiding O(log n) overhead on every update.

2. **Batch Top-K Extraction**: Cache the current top-K result and only recompute when updates affect users in the top-K, reducing query frequency from every batch to only when necessary.

## Comparison to Rebuilding
Rebuilding from scratch would require O(n) time per batch to sort all users, resulting in O(n × batches) total time. Our approach achieves O(B log n) updates and O(K log n) queries, providing significant improvement when K << n and B is moderate.

## Complexity Analysis
- **Initialization**: O(n) - one insertion per user
- **Batch Updates**: O(B log n) - each update requires remove/insert operations
- **Top-K Query**: O(K log n) - traverse at most K score buckets
- **Space**: O(n) - store user-score mappings and score buckets

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design incremental data structures that handle batch updates with strong worst-case guarantees, and to reason about amortized costs versus rebuilding from scratch.

## Key Concepts
- DSU with parity (union-find with bipartite tracking)
- Incremental graph property maintenance
- Amortized analysis (inverse Ackermann function)
- Batch processing optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
class ParityDSU {
  constructor(n) {
    this.parent = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => i);
    this.parity = new Array(n).fill(0);
    this.rank = new Array(n).fill(0);
    this.isBipartite = true;
  }

  find(x) {
    if (this.parent[x] !== x) {
      const root = this.find(this.parent[x]);
      this.parity[x] ^= this.parity[this.parent[x]];
      this.parent[x] = root;
    }
    return this.parent[x];
  }

  union(x, y) {
    const rootX = this.find(x);
    const rootY = this.find(y);
    if (rootX === rootY) {
      if (this.parity[x] === this.parity[y]) {
        this.isBipartite = false;
      }
      return;
    }
    if (this.rank[rootX] < this.rank[rootY]) {
      this.parent[rootX] = rootY;
      this.parity[rootX] = this.parity[x] ^ this.parity[y] ^ 1;
    } else if (this.rank[rootX] > this.rank[rootY]) {
      this.parent[rootY] = rootX;
      this.parity[rootY] = this.parity[x] ^ this.parity[y] ^ 1;
    } else {
      this.parent[rootY] = rootX;
      this.rank[rootX]++;
      this.parity[rootY] = this.parity[x] ^ this.parity[y] ^ 1;
    }
  }

  processBatch(edges) {
    for (const [u, v] of edges) {
      this.union(u, v);
    }
    return this.isBipartite;
  }
}
```

## Comparison to Recomputing from Scratch
- **DSU with parity**: O(B α(n)) per batch, O(1) query
- **BFS/DFS recomputation**: O(n + m) per batch

The DSU approach provides a significant speedup when B << m, as it reuses information from previous batches rather than recomputing the entire graph structure.

